Ionic conduction happens only when ions are free to move. Therefore, this type of electric conduction only happens either in molten ionic salts or in salt solutions. Solutions as metal conductors obey Ohm's Law and the resistance ($R$) of an ionic conductor can be computed using the same law. It is convenient to speak in terms of conductance ($G$)--the inverse of resistance. The SI unit of resistance is the Ohm ($\Omega$), whereas the corresponding unit for conductance is the Siemens ($S$), the inverse of the Ohm. In a metallic conductor, resistance depends on the conductor's length ($l$) and section ($A$). It also depends on the characteristics of the metal in terms of its resistivity ($\rho$).
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\text{R} =  \rho\frac{l}{A}  }
\label{formula:resistance}
\end{equation}
In terms of conductance we have that this property depends on the inverse of the resistivity which is called the conductivity ($\kappa$)
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\text{G} =  \kappa\frac{l}{A}  }
\label{formula:conductance}
\end{equation}
The Si units for $\kappa$ are S$\cdot \text{m}^{-1}$ also written as $\Omega\cdot \text{m}^{-1}$. Regularly it is expressed as $\text{m}\Omega\cdot \text{cm}^{-1}$ or $\mu\Omega\cdot \text{cm}^{-1}$.

